","To find the distance when B catches a, we first need to determine the distance A has traveled in the first 3 hours.

A's speed is 10 kmph, so in 3 hours, A will have covered:

Distance = Speed × Time
Distance = 10 kmph × 3 hours
Distance = 30 km

Now, B starts at this point.

Now, we need to find out how long it will take B to catch A.

The relative speed of B relative to A is the difference of their speeds since B is going against A:

Relative speed = B's speed - A's speed
Relative speed = 20 kmph - 10 kmph
Relative speed = 10 kmph

Now, we can find out how long it will take B to cover the 30 km distance A has traveled:

Time to catch A = Distance A has traveled / Relative speed
Time to catch A = 30 km / 10 kmph
Time take for B to catch A = 3 hours

Now, we can find the distance B will traveled in this time:

Distance by B = B's speed × Time taken
Distance by B = 20 kmph × 3 hours
Distance by B = 60 km

Since B starts after A, we need to find the total distance from the starting point when B catches A.

The distance A has traveled in 3 hours + the additional distance A travels in the next 3 hours = Total distance from the starting point when B catches A.

Additional distance A travels in 3 hours = A's speed × Time taken
Additional distance A travels in 3 hours = 10 kmph × 3 hours
Additional distance a travels in 3 hours = 30 km

Total distance from the starting point when B catches A = 30 km (distance A traveled in the first 3 hours) + 30 km ( additional distance A travels in the next 3 hours)
Total distance from the starting point when B catches A = 60 km

Therefore, B catches A by a distance of $\boxed{60}$  km from the starting point.",relevance,"# Task Description
