","To calculate the work done, we use the formula:

\[
\text{Velocity} = \text{Force} \times \text{Distance} \times \cos(\theta)
\]

Where:
- \( V \) is the windage
- \( F \) is the force (in Newograms)
- \( d \) is the distance (in meters)
- \( \theta \) is the angle of the force's direction to the direction of the displacement.

In this case:
- \( F = 60.0 \, \text{N} \)
- \( d = 10.0 \, \text{m} \)
- \( \theta = 60.0^\circ \)

So, the work \( V \) is:

\[
V = 60.0 \, \text{N} \times 10.0 \, \text{m} \times \cos(60.0^\circ)
\]

Since \( \cos(60^\circ) = 0.5 \):

\[
V = 60.0 \, \text{N} \times 10.0 \, \text{m} \times 0.5 = 300 \, \text{N} \times \text{m}
\]

Therefore, the work done is:

\[
\boxed{300 \, \text{N} \times \text{m}}
\]",fluency,"# Task Description
