     \[
     \overline{h} = 0.664 \left( \frac{30.065 \times 10^{-3}}{0.2} \right) (40,000)^{0.5} (0.693)^{0.33} = 17.67 \, \text{W/m}^2\text{K}
     \]  
   - The heat transfer rate \( Q \) is:  
     \[
     Q = \overline{h} \cdot A \cdot \Delta T = 17.67 \times (0.2 \times 0.1) \times (100 - 50) = 17.67 \, \text{W}
     \]  

2. **Percentage Change in Heat Transfer:**  
   - When the velocity is doubled and the pressure is increased to 5 atm, the density \( \rho \) increases by a factor of 5 (since \( \rho \propto P \) at constant temperature).  
